**Definition 26.1 (comparison tasks and their complete alphabets).**
Retain exactly Definition 1.2 and Assumption 1.3: ordered alpha/beta
sources, the substitution in (GDB.1), all four replies, one stationary
actual source, and the charged original-root literal interface. In
particular

$$
\mathcal F_D=\{\langle\alpha,v\rangle:\operatorname{ht}(v)\le D\},
\qquad
\mathcal P_D=\{p:p\text{ starts with }R,\ 1\le|p|\le D\},
\qquad |\mathcal P_D|=2^D-1.
\tag{GDB.102}
$$

For a fixed nonempty public $Q\subseteq\mathcal P_D$, let $M_D(Q)$
be the minimum complete attained alphabet under that same contract,
with only the later request menu restricted to $Q$. Preparation may
depend on $D,Q$ and common installed tables, but not on which request
in $Q$ is supplied after export. It starts source-blind, acquires every
source distinction by paid Reads, and terminates on every original
source. Every surviving source-dependent control, selector, buffer,
length, allocation and accessible timing distinction belongs to its
complete name. The paid export, entry, continuity, access-isolation
and authentication services are precisely those of Assumption 1.3.

After export the common deterministic reader must serve every $p\in Q$
on every $t\in\mathcal F_D$, using zero Reads or one fresh Read at
any finite original-root word chosen from the name, $D,p$ and public
tables before its reply and every supplied count. Its final predictor
must return $r_{\rho^kt}(p)$ for every subsequently supplied authentic
$k\ge0$, with no further Reads. Earlier preparation transcripts are
inaccessible. Thus

$$
M_D(\mathcal P_D)=M_1(D).
\tag{GDB.103}
$$

The restricted tasks are comparisons, rather than alternative
interpretations of $M_1(D)$. For a singleton menu its sole possible
request is already public; this does not grant a full-menu preparer
knowledge of its later request. No competitor is restricted to prefix
queries, product codes, independent children or any fixed source shape.
Preparation charges, installed tables, local work and workspace,
post-request space, final predictor, address costs and the paid services
remain separate from the optimized attained alphabet.

Put, for integers $n\ge1$ and $1\le k\le2^D-1$,

$$
\begin{aligned}
m(n)&=\left\lceil\frac{2n+1}{4}\right\rceil
       =\left\lfloor\frac n2\right\rfloor+1,\\
H_D(k)&=\max_{\substack{\varnothing\ne Q\subseteq\mathcal P_D\\|Q|\le k}}
                 M_D(Q),\qquad
R_{\rm a}=\frac{7792603}{3038031}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{GDB.104}
$$

The ratio $R_{\rm a}$ is the credited [NCE, Theorem 17.1][NCE]
ratio; it is not a new pressure calculation. All logarithms in these
sections have base two. The constructions below establish finite
nonempty classes of lawful schemes, so the positive integer alphabet
minima exist. The maximum defining $H_D(k)$ is over a finite set.

